# =============================================================================
# ODL LoanFlow CRM — environment variables
# =============================================================================
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values. NEVER commit `.env.local`, and
# never put a real value in THIS file — it is the only env file that is
# deliberately tracked in git (see .gitignore), precisely because it contains
# nothing sensitive.
#
# Three categories below:
#   BROWSER-SAFE  — inlined into the client bundle at build time. Anything
#                   prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is public. Never put a secret behind
#                   that prefix.
#   SERVER-ONLY   — read exclusively in Server Components / Server Actions /
#                   Route Handlers. Never referenced from a "use client" file.
#   OPTIONAL      — the app runs without it, with a specific documented
#                   degradation.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — BROWSER-SAFE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Project URL and the publishable (anon) key. Both are shipped to the browser
# by design. The publishable key grants nothing on its own: every table in this
# schema has RLS enabled, and the only policy that exists lets an authenticated
# user read their OWN profile row. All CRM data access happens server-side.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=your-publishable-anon-key


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — SERVER-ONLY. TREAT AS A PASSWORD.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The service-role secret. It BYPASSES Row Level Security completely and is the
# credential every server-side read and write in this app uses.
#
# Referenced in exactly one file — src/lib/supabase/server.ts, which is marked
# `server-only` so importing it from a client component is a build error.
#
# NEVER expose this through a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable. NEVER paste it into a
# client component, a log line, or an error message.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=your-service-role-secret


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DeepL — SERVER-ONLY, OPTIONAL (degraded mode)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Powers internal-chat message translation.
#
# DEGRADATION IF ABSENT: the app builds, boots and runs normally. Chat still
# sends and stores messages; translation returns an explicit "not configured"
# result which /api/translate maps to its own status code. Nothing crashes and
# no message is lost — translated text simply is not produced.
DEEPL_API_KEY=your-deepl-api-key


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ODL mailbox (IMAP + SMTP) — SERVER-ONLY, OPTIONAL (degraded mode)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Milestone 26B-9A. ODL's operational mailbox on cPanel. The CRM speaks IMAP to
# the mail server directly; Roundcube is only the webmail UI in front of the
# same mailbox and continues to work untouched.
#
# Read in exactly one place — src/lib/config/mail.ts — which is marked
# `server-only`, so importing it from a client component is a build error and
# the password cannot reach the browser bundle.
#
# DEGRADATION IF ABSENT: the app builds, boots and runs normally. The Correo /
# Email page still renders from previously synchronised messages in the CRM
# database; the Sync button reports "not configured" instead of connecting.
# Nothing crashes and no stored mail is lost.
#
# ODL_EMAIL_PASSWORD IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY HERE AND MUST STAY EMPTY. This file
# is the one env file tracked in git. Put the real value in `.env.local` only,
# which .gitignore excludes.
ODL_EMAIL_ADDRESS=prestamo@odlfinanciera.com

ODL_IMAP_HOST=odlfinanciera.com
ODL_IMAP_PORT=993
ODL_IMAP_SECURE=true

# SMTP is CONFIGURATION ONLY in 26B-9A — no transport is installed and nothing
# is sent. Present so 26B-9B adds outbound behind a reviewed surface.
ODL_SMTP_HOST=odlfinanciera.com
ODL_SMTP_PORT=465
ODL_SMTP_SECURE=true

ODL_EMAIL_PASSWORD=


# =============================================================================
# MANUAL PRODUCTION CONFIGURATION — NOT environment variables
# =============================================================================
#
# The following must be configured in the Supabase and hosting dashboards.
# Setting the variables above is NOT sufficient for a production deployment.
# (Identified by the Milestone 22 pre-go-live audit.)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Supabase Auth — Site URL
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#    Set to the production origin (e.g. https://crm.example.com).
#    Used as the base for auth email links.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Supabase Auth — Redirect allow-list
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#    Must include the production callback:
#
#        https://<production-domain>/auth/callback
#
#    Staff invitations and password resets both redirect through
#    `${origin}/auth/callback?next=/reset-password`. The origin is derived from
#    the incoming request, so it is correct per environment automatically — but
#    Supabase REJECTS any redirect target not on this allow-list. If the
#    production URL is missing here, invitations fail and the invited person
#    lands on an error page.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Production SMTP — REQUIRED BEFORE STAFF ONBOARDING
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#    Supabase's built-in SMTP is rate-limited and explicitly not intended for
#    production. Every staff invitation and every password reset depends on
#    mail delivery. Configure a real SMTP provider under
#    Authentication -> Emails -> SMTP Settings before inviting anyone.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Hosting environment variables (e.g. Vercel)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#    All four variables above must be set for the production environment.
#    SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY and DEEPL_API_KEY must NOT be exposed to the browser
#    build — keep them out of any NEXT_PUBLIC_ name.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Public intake endpoint — BEFORE external website integration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#    POST /api/public/application-intake is live and unauthenticated. Today it
#    is protected by a same-origin check, a honeypot field, and strict payload
#    validation. Before ODL's real website is pointed at it:
#
#      a. Agree the allowed origin(s). The current check requires SAME origin,
#         so a website on a different domain WILL be rejected until this is
#         explicitly decided and implemented.
#      b. Implement platform rate limiting (e.g. Vercel Firewall). There is
#         deliberately no in-process limiter — it would reset per cold start
#         and give false confidence.
#      c. Publish the intake payload contract for the website developer.
#      d. Decide the document-upload architecture. The endpoint accepts
#         applicant data only; it does not accept file uploads.
#
#    None of (a)-(d) is implemented. See the Milestone 22 audit, section 7.
